is equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=5,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-1W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=5{e}^{2}{e}^{-x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186652941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6873c603-ef4c-59ba-adf4-98615b0d8e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186652561} {:answer/id #uuid "8c66b96f-4c2e-5dfe-907e-2542720d4ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186652615} {:answer/id #uuid "a106e74e-97e4-57cb-ac9d-0080fef829b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186652644} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf0fa54-a002-5ea6-a7df-f5dd2162653f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/ma